By the end of this tutorial you will have a small Astro project with a list of posts and a tag filter. It is deliberately tiny so the steps stay easy to follow, and it doubles as a showcase for the code blocks on this site.

## Before you start

You will need:

- Node.js 22 or newer
- A package manager, this guide uses `pnpm`
- A terminal and an editor you like

{% table %}
- Tool
- Version
- Check with
---
- Node.js
- 22.12 or newer
- `node -v`
---
- pnpm
- 10 or newer
- `pnpm -v`
{% /table %}

## Step 1: create the project

Scaffold a new site and answer the prompts.

```bash
pnpm create astro@latest my-site
```

### Install dependencies

Move into the folder and install everything. The copy button in the top corner of each block copies the commands without the prompt.

```bash
cd my-site
pnpm install
```

### Start the dev server

```bash
pnpm dev
```

The site is now on `http://localhost:4321`. Leave it running while you work; edits show up instantly.

## Step 2: describe a post

Add a type for a post and a helper that filters by tag. The title appears in the corner of the block, and the highlighted line is the one doing the work.

```ts {% title="src/lib/posts.ts" mark="7" %}
type TypePost = {
  title: string;
  tags: string[];
};

export function byTag(posts: TypePost[], tag: string) {
  return posts.filter((post) => post.tags.includes(tag));
}
```

### Handle a missing tag

Not every post is tagged, so make the helper return everything when no tag is given. Added lines are green and removed lines are red.

```ts {% title="src/lib/posts.ts" del="1" ins="2-3" %}
export function byTag(posts: TypePost[], tag: string) {
export function byTag(posts: TypePost[], tag?: string) {
  if (!tag) return posts;
  return posts.filter((post) => post.tags.includes(tag));
}
```

## Step 3: render the list

Use the helper in a page. Long lines scroll sideways by default; press the wrap button in the corner to wrap them instead.

```astro {% title="src/pages/index.astro" wrap=true %}
---
import { byTag } from "../lib/posts";

const posts = [
  { title: "Hello world, a post with a rather long title that would normally force this block to scroll sideways", tags: ["astro"] },
  { title: "Styling with Tailwind", tags: ["tailwind"] },
];
const filtered = byTag(posts, Astro.url.searchParams.get("tag") ?? undefined);
---

<ul>
  {filtered.map((post) => <li>{post.title}</li>)}
</ul>
```

Check your work:

1. Open the home page, both posts should appear
2. Add `?tag=astro` to the address, only the first remains
3. Add `?tag=nothing`, the list is empty

## Wrap up

You built a small tag filter, and along the way saw titled, highlighted and diffed code blocks in action. From here, try moving the posts into a content collection and generating one page per tag.
